# Generated output. A line-level merge of it produces a file that describes
# neither side — this merge produced seven such hunks, each one a pair of
# element counts and geometry hashes spliced into a value that was never
# measured from any tree.
#
# `-merge` makes git stop trying: the conflict is declared, our side is kept,
# and the correct resolution is always the same one — regenerate it.
#
#   npm run snap          rewrite it from the current code
#   npm run snap --check   confirm it still describes the code
#
# Regenerate only when every third-party request has a fixture. If snap reports
# requests that "went to the network", the values it writes for those screens
# are measurements of live TMDB and will drift for reasons nobody caused, which
# is the thing pinning the corpus exists to prevent.
snapshots/fingerprints.json -merge -diff

# Recorded corpus: compressed, so there is nothing for git to merge or show.
snapshots/tmdb-fixtures.json.gz binary
